  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 930 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Wednesday Morning: Periodic Rain and Storms\n\nThe line of showers and storms along a cold front that moved \nout of central MN early this morning faded by 9am as the\nattendant upper wave lifted into SW Ontario. The upcoming rain \nfocus shifts to mainly south of I-90 for this afternoon and \ntonight as the lower tropospheric baroclinic zone/theta-e axis \nsags into Iowa and far southern Wisconsin (with convergence \nstrongest in Iowa) and the right-rear quadrant of the upper \nlevel jet arrives. \n\nUpstream radar trends over Nebraska and western Iowa continue \nto support that the highest rainfall amounts will be south of \nthe forecast area, with lighter stratiform precipitation \npossibly advecting as far north as I-90 as we go through the \nafternoon and overnight. Latest HRRR runs support observational \ntrends and take much of the heavier precipitation closer to I-80\nlate tonight. Could still pick up a quick 0.5-0.75 inches of \nrain in pockets along and south of Highway 18 before Wednesday \nmorning given the ample deep moisture present, but otherwise the\nimpacts from this rain look to be minimal.\n\nThursday - Friday: Seasonable and Mostly Dry\n\nBy Thursday, surface high pressure builds in, giving the area a \nbreak from any precipitation chances. On Friday another shortwave \ntrough comes down into the Upper Midwest and while most of the day \nappears to be dry, there is increased precipitation chances (10 to \n30%), however with increased southwest flow in the low levels, there \nis lower confidence on any surface forcing that would allow \nprecipitation to reach the ground. With the southwest flow, \ntemperatures will also increase a little, more towards the upper 70s \nto upper 80s. \n\nThis Weekend: Increasing Warmth with Additional Storm Chances \n\nBy the time the weekend rolls around, zonal to northwesterly flow \nestablishes itself into the region with continued southwesterly \nsurface flow. Consequently, temperatures are expected to steadily \nwarm through the weekend with the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble) inter-quartile spread ranging from the middle 80s to near \n90F by Sunday for high temperatures across the region. With the \nzonal to northwesterly flow in place, the key question that will \nneed to be monitored for over the weekend is how far northeast does \nthe ridge situated west of the local area build. Currently, there \nare some respectable probabilities (20-40%) for 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE \nand 30 kts of shear, so there is certainly some potential for severe \nweather when also considering AI guidance (CSU/Pangu/FengWu) does \npaint some probabilities (5-15%) as well. That being said, it will \nbe highly contingent on how quickly the ridge builds in, should it \nprogress into the region faster, this would decrease any convective \npotential. Regardless, thinking the low to medium-end precipitation \nprobabilities (20-50%) in the NBM for this weekend seem appropriate \ngiven the uncertainty of how this weekend may evolve.",
